markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>The Pieces of a Function</h1>

<p>You just saw why <code>addPoints()</code> was worth creating: one place to update instead of several. Now let's look at how that function is actually built, piece by piece.</p>

<p>Every function you'll see this unit follows the same shape: a name, an arrow, a body, and somewhere else in the code, a call. Below, you can click through each part of <code>addPoints</code> to see exactly what job it's doing, then try it running for real.</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
<p>📖 <b>Function call</b> is the moment a function actually runs. Defining a function, giving it a name and a body, doesn't run it by itself. It only runs once something calls it, like a button's click triggering <code>addPoints</code>.</p>
</div>

---

<div style="width:100%; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden; margin:20px 0;">
