</div>


---

<h2>Abstraction</h2>

<p>Notice that calling <code>addPoints</code> in the demo didn't require rereading what's inside it, the name alone was enough to know what would happen.</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
<p>📖 <b>Abstraction</b> is hiding complexity behind a name, so something can be used without needing to see how it works underneath.</p>
</div><br>

<p>That's exactly why the function's name matters as much as its body. A well-named function lets anyone, a teammate, a future you, or AI reading the code, trust what it does without opening it up to check.</p>
MARKDOWN
